UID:studios-at-mass-moca-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Studios at MASS MoCA Residencies
DESCRIPTION:The Studios at MASS MoCA is a year-round residency program on t
 he museum's campus in North Adams\, Massachusetts\, hosting up to 10 artis
 ts at a time for stays of two or four weeks. It primarily welcomes visual 
 artists across all media and writers in all literary disciplines\, and app
 licants from around the world are encouraged to apply. Residents receive 2
 4-hour access to a private furnished studio\, lodging in nearby apartments
 \, and several communal meals each week\, plus museum membership benefits 
 and access to The Clark Art Institute. The program offers need-based finan
 cial aid (artists typically pay between USD 0 and USD 450 per week) and fu
 lly funded fellowships that cover all residency fees for up to four weeks.
  General application deadlines fall on May 1 and November 1 each year\, an
 d the application is free to submit.\n\nOrganisation: MASS MoCA (Massachus
 etts Museum of Contemporary Art)\nLocation: North Adams\, Massachusetts\, 
 USA\nAward: Provides private 24/7 studio space\, furnished housing\, and s
 even communal meals per week. Financial aid is available so artists reques
 ting it typically pay between USD 0 and USD 450 per week\, and fully funde
 d fellowships cover all residency fees for up to four weeks. Artists who f
 ully self-fund pay a subsidized rate of USD 650 per week.\nDuration: 2 or 
 4 weeks\n\nMore: https://massmoca.org/studios/?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://massmoca.org/studios/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
LOCATION:North Adams\, Massachusetts\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kala-fellowship-program-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270301
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270302
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kala Fellowship Program
DESCRIPTION:The Kala Fellowship Program supports international\, national\,
  and local artists producing innovative work in and across multiple media\
 , with an emphasis on Kala's specialized printmaking\, photography\, and d
 igital media equipment. The institute supports up to 6 artists per cycle w
 ith a USD 3\,000 stipend\, 24/7 access to its Berkeley facilities for 1 to
  9 months\, one month of housing for artists based outside the 9 Bay Area 
 counties\, a Kala class or workshop\, and a culminating group show in the 
 Kala Gallery. Applications are submitted through kala.slideroom.com and re
 quire a statement of interest\, an artist CV\, documentation of 5 to 10 re
 cent works\, and two references. Artists enrolled in a degree-granting pro
 gram during the fellowship period are not eligible. The application opens 
 in December with no fee through January 15\, after which a USD 25 applicat
 ion fee applies through the March 1 deadline.\n\nOrganisation: Kala Art In
 stitute\nLocation: Berkeley\, California\, USA\nAward: USD 3\,000 stipend\
 , 24/7 facility access for 1 to 9 months\, 1 month of housing for artists 
 outside the 9 Bay Area counties\, a Kala class or workshop\, and a group e
 xhibition USD 25 application fee (free if applying December through Januar
 y 15)\nDuration: 1 to 9 months within the July 2026 to June 2027 fellowshi
 p period\n\nMore: https://www.kala.org/residencies/fellowship-program/?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.kala.org/residencies/fellowship-program/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berkeley\, California\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:justice-joy-summer-research-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260709
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260710
SUMMARY:Deadline: Justice + Joy Summer Research Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Justice + Joy Research Fellowship is a seven-week virtual s
 ummer program for girls\, young women\, and gender-expansive young people 
 of color (ages 18-26) in the United States who want to explore issues impa
 cting their lives\, communities\, and futures. Fellows identify a topic th
 ey care about\, such as mental health\, education\, reproductive justice\,
  immigration\, AI and technology\, gender-based violence\, beauty standard
 s\, young parenthood\, or another issue affecting their communities\, and 
 develop an original research project grounded in their experiences\, quest
 ions\, and ideas. Throughout the summer\, fellows participate in weekly vi
 rtual sessions\, engage with guest speakers\, build community with peers f
 rom across the country\, and receive mentorship and support as they develo
 p their research. The fellowship culminates in a capstone showcase where f
 ellows present their work to the Justice + Joy community\, including organ
 izational leaders\, partners\, and supporters. No prior research experienc
 e is required\; the program seeks young people who are curious\, committed
  to learning\, and interested in using research as a tool for understandin
 g\, advocacy\, and change. COMMITMENT: weekly virtual sessions on Wednesda
 ys 5:00-6:30 PM ET (9 July - 20 August 2026)\; approximately 2-4 hours of 
 independent work per week\; required orientation on Wednesday 9 July\; dev
 elopment of an individual research proposal or conceptual framework\; pres
 entation at the capstone showcase (tentatively in person\, mid-September 2
 026\, with all travel and lodging covered). WHAT YOU GET: a USD 2\,000 sti
 pend paid upon successful completion\; skills-based training in research m
 ethods\, social justice frameworks\, and community advocacy\; guest speake
 r sessions\; a peer community\; and a real audience for the research (the 
 Justice + Joy board and funders). Apply via the Google Form. Note: an expl
 icit application deadline is not stated\; the required orientation and pro
 gram start is 9 July 2026\, so apply before then.\n\nOrganisation: Justice
  + Joy\nLocation: United States\; fully virtual program. Capstone showcase
  tentatively in person (mid-September 2026)\, with travel and lodging cove
 red.\nAward: USD 2\,000 stipend paid upon successful program completion\, 
 plus skills-based training\, guest speaker sessions\, and peer community. 
 All travel and lodging for the in-person capstone are covered.\nDuration: 
 Seven-week virtual program\, 9 July to 20 August 2026\, with a capstone sh
 owcase mid-September 2026. Weekly Wednesday sessions 5:00-6:30 PM ET plus 
 approximately 2-4 hours of independent work per week.\n\nMore: https://doc
 s.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfXN_oqSA7G7RRPjhLp-_McFix70m4g8Q_yny0aiBDb-
 qfHvw/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfXN_oqSA7G7RRPjhLp-_McFix70m4
 g8Q_yny0aiBDb-qfHvw/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:United States\; fully virtual program. Capstone showcase tentative
 ly in person (mid-September 2026)\, with travel and lodging covered.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:genesis-mission-ai-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Genesis Mission AI Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Horizon Institute for Public Service\, Renaissance Philanth
 ropy\, SeedAI\, and Fulcrum Science are partnering to launch a fellowship 
 that embeds technical AI talent across the US Department of Energy nationa
 l labs to advance the goals of the Genesis Mission\, DOE's flagship scienc
 e and technology initiative launched in late 2025. The Genesis Mission ide
 ntifies 26 national science and technology challenges critical to US leade
 rship and envisions a national platform connecting supercomputers\, experi
 mental facilities\, AI systems\, and unique datasets\, with DOE's 17 natio
 nal labs playing a key role. Genesis Mission AI Fellows are forward deploy
 ed to DOE national labs to bring frontier AI expertise to high-priority sc
 ientific challenges. As core members of the technical staff on a Genesis M
 ission project\, fellows work alongside national lab scientists and partne
 rs and apply AI to national priorities\, from building cutting-edge models
  of plasma dynamics in pursuit of controlled fusion to advancing state-of-
 the-art models for foundational materials discovery. Initial national lab 
 partners include Idaho National Laboratory\, Brookhaven National Laborator
 y\, and the Princeton Plasma Physics Laboratory\; additional partners may 
 be announced later. COMPENSATION/DURATION: 9-12 months starting fall 2026\
 , with a prorated total annual compensation of USD 200\,000\; fellows are 
 expected to travel to their national lab site at least once a quarter but 
 may work on-site if they prefer. ELIGIBILITY: competitive applicants shoul
 d have at least 2 years of industry experience building AI models or agent
 s (those with industry experience building AI applications or data infrast
 ructure will also be considered)\; scientific domain expertise is not requ
 ired\, but any placement will involve some training and study in a specifi
 c scientific discipline or energy topic\, supported by expert colleagues a
 t the host institution. All fellows must be eligible to work in the United
  States for the duration of the placement\; US employment visas cannot be 
 sponsored\, but US permanent residents (green card holders) are eligible. 
 APPLICATION: initial form requires a CV or resume (max 2 pages) and short-
 answer questions on motivations and experiences (max 200 words)\; successf
 ul applicants complete interviews through partner organizations and nation
 al labs\, and selected fellows are matched to partner labs by scientific d
 omain and availability. The review uses evidence-based best practices incl
 uding blind grading of application materials. TIMELINE: application deadli
 ne 31 July 2026\; final fellow selection August-September 2026\; expected 
 start fall 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Horizon Institute for Public Service (wi
 th Renaissance Philanthropy\, SeedAI\, and Fulcrum Science)\nLocation: Uni
 ted States. Fellows are forward deployed to US Department of Energy nation
 al labs (initial partners: Idaho National Laboratory\, Brookhaven National
  Laboratory\, Princeton Plasma Physics Laboratory). Remote or in-person\; 
 on-site travel at least once a quarter. Must be eligible to work in the US
  (no visa sponsorship\; US green card holders eligible).\nAward: Prorated 
 total annual compensation of USD 200\,000.\nDuration: 9-12 months\, starti
 ng fall 2026.\n\nMore: https://horizonpublicservice.org/announcing-the-gen
 esis-mission-ai-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://horizonpublicservice.org/announcing-the-genesis-mission-ai-fell
 owship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States. Fellows are forward deployed to US Department of En
 ergy national labs (initial partners: Idaho National Laboratory\, Brookhav
 en National Laboratory\, Princeton Plasma Physics Laboratory). Remote or i
 n-person\; on-site travel at least once a quarter. Must be eligible to wor
 k in the US (no visa sponsorship\; US green card holders eligible).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:frame-fellowship-2-ai-safety-content-creators-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260710
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260711
SUMMARY:Deadline: Frame Fellowship 2.0 (Residential Accelerator for AI Safe
 ty Content Creators) 2026
DESCRIPTION:Frame Fellowship is a 10-week residential accelerator in San Fr
 ancisco for content creators educating the public on AI safety. Frame's pr
 emise is that content creators can solve a long-standing bottleneck of exp
 anding reach and trust beyond the AI safety and effective altruism (EA) bu
 bble\; creators today hack attention\, empower mass action and shape cultu
 re. Past Frame Fellows include award-winning filmmakers\, journalists from
  leading newsrooms and ex-activists who transitioned to educating\, empowe
 ring and preparing their communities for the impacts of AGI. Frame helps a
 mplify their impact to millions of people\, connects them to funding and s
 ponsorship opportunities at organizations such as FLI\, CAIS and Seismic\,
  and helps establish their path as a leading content creator. This cohort 
 runs 10 weeks with a stronger mentor and creator network\, access to produ
 ction-ready studios in San Francisco\, housing\, food and a stipend\, plus
  connections to funding and partnership opportunities after the program. T
 WO TRACKS: (1) Independent Track\, build your own channel and become a def
 ining voice on a topic\; (2) Amplifier Track\, embed with an AI org/compan
 y and grow their reach. SUPPORT: Frame covers accommodation\, meals\, flig
 hts and a USD 10\,000 stipend for the 10 weeks so fellows can go all in\; 
 top performers may be eligible for additional grant opportunities beyond t
 he program. Early deadline: 10 July 2026. Apply at https://framefellowship
 .com/.\n\nOrganisation: Frame Fellowship\nLocation: San Francisco\, USA\; 
 residential (in person).\nAward: USD 10\,000 stipend for the 10 weeks\, pl
 us accommodation\, meals and flights covered. Access to production-ready s
 tudios in San Francisco\, a mentor and creator network\, and connections t
 o funding\, sponsorship and partnership opportunities at organizations suc
 h as FLI\, CAIS and Seismic. Top performers may be eligible for additional
  grant opportunities beyond the program. No application fee.\nDuration: 10
 -week residential program\n\nMore: https://framefellowship.com/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://framefellowship.com/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
LOCATION:San Francisco\, USA\; residential (in person).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gmu-civic-science-fellow-science-policy-collaborative-2026@artificialno
 uveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260705
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260706
SUMMARY:Deadline: Civic Science Postdoctoral Fellow: Science Policy Collabo
 rative (George Mason University) 2026
DESCRIPTION:A grant-funded\, full-time Civic Science Postdoctoral Fellowshi
 p supporting the Science Policy Collaborative\, a national network that st
 rengthens the development\, sustainability and evaluation of U.S. science 
 policy programs. The fellow helps shape an emerging field at the heart of 
 a growing community of practice connecting science policy programs across 
 the country. WHAT YOU'LL DO: conduct a national needs assessment of U.S. s
 cience policy programs and publish the findings\; support the Collaborativ
 e's working groups by scoping deliverables and building partnerships to ex
 ecute them\; develop infrastructure for knowledge exchange (tools like log
 ic models and evaluation frameworks)\; connect programs with national and 
 international experts on the use of research evidence (URE) and evidence-i
 nformed policymaking\; present at partner meetings and convenings\; and re
 ceive mentorship from George Mason University\, UC Riverside and other lea
 ders of the Collaborative. WHO THEY'RE LOOKING FOR: someone with a doctora
 l degree\, relevant experience\, knowledge of science-for-policy programs\
 , and skills in network coordination\, needs assessment or program evaluat
 ion\, and writing for broad audiences (see the posting for exact criteria)
 . Fairfax\, VA (hybrid eligible)\; full-time 18-month postdoctoral appoint
 ment issued as two contracts (12 months plus 6 months)\; salary about USD 
 80\,000/year plus benefits. APPLY with a cover letter\, CV\, three referen
 ces and a writing sample written for broad audiences\; for full considerat
 ion apply by 5 July 2026 (open until filled). Questions to search coordina
 tor Natalie V Lapidot-Croitoru\, nlapidot@gmu.edu.\n\nOrganisation: George
  Mason University (with UC Riverside)\, via the Science Policy Collaborati
 ve and Civic Science Fellows network\nLocation: Fairfax\, Virginia\, USA (
 hybrid eligible).\nAward: Salary approximately USD 80\,000 per year plus b
 enefits. No application fee.\nDuration: 18-month postdoctoral appointment 
 (two contracts: 12 months + 6 months)\n\nMore: https://listings.jobs.gmu.e
 du/jobs/civic-science-fellow-science-policy-collaborative-fairfax-va-virgi
 nia-united-states-other?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://listings.jobs.gmu.edu/jobs/civic-science-fellow-science-policy-
 collaborative-fairfax-va-virginia-united-states-other?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fairfax\, Virginia\, USA (hybrid eligible).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:harvard-radcliffe-institute-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: Harvard Radcliffe Institute: Fellowship Program 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:The Harvard Radcliffe Institute Fellowship offers scientists\, 
 writers\, scholars\, public intellectuals\, and artists a year to pursue a
 mbitious projects in a vibrant interdisciplinary setting amid the resource
 s of Harvard University. The Institute welcomes innovative work that confr
 onts pressing social\, scientific\, and policy issues and seeks to engage 
 audiences beyond academia. Reflecting Radcliffe's history\, it welcomes (b
 ut does not limit eligibility to) proposals focused on women\, gender\, an
 d society\, or that draw on the Schlesinger Library's collections. It also
  invites proposals relevant to the Institute's 2024-2029 focus area\, acad
 emic freedom and connecting across difference (intellectual virtues\, free
  and open inquiry\, diversity of thought\, political polarization\, peace 
 and conflict\, inequality\, religious pluralism\, and related higher-educa
 tion policy issues)\, including work that constructively challenges discip
 linary orthodoxies or advances transformative perspectives. SUPPORT: USD 7
 8\,000 stipend plus USD 5\,000 for project expenses\, with relocation\, ho
 using\, and childcare funds available\; fellows must reside in the Cambrid
 ge/Boston area from September through May. ELIGIBILITY: open to individual
 s across career stages\; tenure is not required and applicants need not be
  academics. This is NOT a postdoctoral fellowship\; those currently enroll
 ed in a degree program are ineligible\, as are former Harvard Radcliffe fe
 llows (1999-present). Applicants must meet discipline-specific criteria. A
 PPLY: register on the online portal and select an area (Humanities and Soc
 ial Sciences\; Creative Arts\; Nonfiction and Journalism\; or Science\, En
 gineering\, and Mathematics). Materials: application form\, CV\, 1\,400-wo
 rd project proposal (with bibliography when appropriate)\, a writing or wo
 rk sample\, and three references. DEADLINES: humanities\, social sciences\
 , creative arts\, and nonfiction and journalism by 10 September 2026\, 5pm
  ET\; science\, engineering\, and mathematics by 1 October 2026\, 5pm ET.\
 n\nOrganisation: Harvard Radcliffe Institute\nLocation: Cambridge\, Massac
 husetts\, USA\; fellows must relocate to and reside in the Cambridge/Bosto
 n area for the full fellowship (September through May).\nAward: USD 78\,00
 0 stipend plus an additional USD 5\,000 for project expenses. Relocation\,
  housing\, and childcare funds may also be provided. No application fee.\n
 Duration: One academic year\, September 2027 through May 2028 (residential
 )\n\nMore: https://www.radcliffe.harvard.edu/radcliffe-fellowship/applicat
 ion-information?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.radcliffe.harvard.edu/radcliffe-fellowship/application-info
 rmation?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, USA\; fellows must relocate to and res
 ide in the Cambridge/Boston area for the full fellowship (September throug
 h May).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lighthouse-works-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261015
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261016
SUMMARY:Deadline: Lighthouse Works: Fellowship Program 2027 (Fishers Island
 )
DESCRIPTION:Lighthouse Works' Fellowship Program supports a diverse range o
 f cultural producers working at the vanguard of their creative fields. Fel
 lowships are six weeks in length\, occur year-round\, and provide fellows 
 with housing\, food\, studio space\, and USD 1\,750 in financial support. 
 Fellows enjoy a private bedroom and share a kitchen\, bathrooms\, and livi
 ng space in a 3-story Victorian house\; all dietary needs are accommodated
 \, and on most nights Lighthouse Works staff cook for and eat dinner with 
 the fellows. Studios are located about 1.5 miles (a 30-minute walk) from t
 he fellowship house\, are private and flooded with light\, and face the oc
 ean adjacent to Silver Eel Cove where the island ferry arrives. Lighthouse
  Works also maintains a wood and metal fabrication shop and a kiln. While 
 in residence\, a fellow's primary obligation is to pursue their own work\,
  though every fellow participates in two events\, an Artist Talk and Open 
 Studio\, that bookend the fellowship\; the program's intimate size allows 
 for conversation\, critique\, and collaboration. Artistic excellence is th
 e primary criterion for acceptance. Artists at any stage of their career a
 re encouraged to apply through the online Slideroom system. SELECTION: sta
 ff review applications for completeness\, a jury of experts in each artist
 's field reviews complete applications and identifies finalists\, intervie
 ws are scheduled in early January\, and applicants are notified in mid-Jan
 uary. The program is supported in part by a grant from the Robert Rauschen
 berg Foundation. APPLICATION CALL: Lighthouse Works accepts applications e
 ach year from September to October (the 2026 portal was open 15 September 
 to 15 October 2025). The deadline shown here is the anticipated close of t
 he next annual call for the 2027 cycle\; confirm exact dates on the Lighth
 ouse Works site when the portal reopens. Apply at http://thelighthousework
 s.slideroom.com.\n\nOrganisation: Lighthouse Works\nLocation: Fishers Isla
 nd\, New York\, USA (in-person residency)\nAward: Six-week fellowship prov
 iding housing\, food\, private studio space\, and USD 1\,750 in financial 
 support. No application fee mentioned.\nDuration: 6 weeks\; fellowships oc
 cur year-round across five sessions\n\nMore: https://lighthouseworks.us/fe
 llowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://lighthouseworks.us/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fishers Island\, New York\, USA (in-person residency)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:zwillgen-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T123517Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: ZwillGen Fellowship Program 2027-2028 (Technology Law)
DESCRIPTION:The ZwillGen Fellowship is a 12-month position for recent law-s
 chool graduates and early-career attorneys interested in working at the in
 tersection of law\, technology\, and public policy. Fellows work alongside
  attorneys on matters involving privacy\, cybersecurity\, artificial intel
 ligence\, online platforms\, surveillance\, digital advertising\, fintech\
 , online safety\, and technology regulation\, contributing to client couns
 eling\, investigations\, litigation\, and emerging regulatory matters\, as
  well as research\, writing\, and speaking. The program includes a reduced
  billable target (1\,250 hours) to make room for thought leadership and en
 gagement with the technology policy\, privacy\, and civil liberties commun
 ity. ELIGIBILITY: must have graduated from law school before the program s
 tarts\; must have taken or plan to take a bar exam near the start of the F
 ellowship\; must be able to work out of the DC office and be eligible to a
 pply for admission to the DC Bar. Candidates primarily interested in tradi
 tional IP practice may find it less aligned. A small number of applicants 
 may also be considered for the Hannah Schaller Memorial Skylark Fellowship
  (privacy-law focus\, with a reduced 1\,100-hour billable target for addit
 ional community engagement). Following the Fellowship\, fellows may be inv
 ited into a full-time attorney role or supported in pursuing other technol
 ogy law and policy opportunities. APPLY with a resume\, law-school transcr
 ipt\, writing sample\, and cover letter by 31 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation
 : ZwillGen\nLocation: Washington\, D.C.\, USA (must work out of the DC off
 ice)\nAward: Salary USD 115\,000 to USD 125\,000\, with a reduced annual b
 illable target (1\,250 hours) to create space for writing\, professional d
 evelopment\, and policy engagement. No application fee.\nDuration: 12-mont
 h position beginning Fall 2027\n\nMore: https://www.zwillgen.com/careers/f
 ellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referr
 al
URL:https://www.zwillgen.com/careers/fellowship-program/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, D.C.\, USA (must work out of the DC office)
END:VEVENT
